          "source_page_number": null,
          "source_image_index": null,
          "ownership": "unknown"
        }
      },
      {
        "part_label": "(f)",
        "part_text": "Insulin and glucagon - Glucagon and insulin are secreted by cells of pancreas. They regulate the blood glucose level in the body. α-cells secrete glucagon that maintain a normal blood glucose level in the body, whereas β-cells secrete insulin that regulates the storage of glycogen in the liver. Function of insulin - Insulin stimulates glycogenesis (conversion of glucose to glycogen). The rapid conversion of glucose from the blood to glycogen in hepatocytes and adipocytes results into a decreased glucose level. Insulin also prevents the formation of glucose from non-carbohydrate substances such as proteins and fats. Hence, it acts as a regulator of carbohydrate metabolism.",
        "part_latex": null,
        "answer_text": "Insulin lowers blood glucose levels, while glucagon raises them.",
        "answer_latex": null,
        "steps": [],
        "final_answer": "Thus, they maintain glucose homeostasis.",
